"Settings","group":null,"lastEventId":null,"order":8,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"text","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"helpText":"The base directory to use for this project","controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text"},"name":"Settings.Project_Directory","group":"Settings","lastEventId":null,"order":29,"lastValue":"/Users/piny/JobApp/workspace"},{"lastDataType":5,"controlType":"anything","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":1237,"currentPath":["{#}LWZxQBT:1237","{#}YgrOhyz","{#}83BpzNj"]},"helpText":"Routes tool scanning events to all agent tools.","controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Settings.Scan _Tools","group":"Settings","lastEventId":null,"order":30,"lastValue":{"id":"toolu_01KCmsmSmH6BRvfy58f1AGzM","type":"function","function":{"name":"ReadFile","arguments":"{\"filePath\": \"research_output.json\"}"}}},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"group","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Agent Outputs","order":1,"group":null,"lastEventId":null,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":593,"currentPath":["{#}2v0z3Wc:1","{#}w4sX2KA","{#}4tWyohj"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.analyst_output","order":2,"group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"lastValue":"I'll execute the gap analysis now. Starting with data load and proceeding through all phases silently.Good. `gap_analysis.json` is empty (re-invocation guard passes). All required files loaded successfully. Proceeding with requirement extraction and analysis.\n\nThe candidate has:\n- Bachelor of Biomedical Science from La Trobe (completed 2023) — matches required qualification\n- Expired First Aid (2022)\n- Retail work history (Bunnings 2024–present, Coles 2020–2024) — no laboratory experience\n- Basic technical skills (Microsoft Word, POS)\n- No laboratory-specific technical skills, LIMS experience, or analyser experience\n\nNow building the full requirements array, matching against candidate profile, and constructing strengths/gaps.The validator tool timed out. Proceeding to read the verdict file directly.The validator has issued a REJECT verdict with 4 blocking issues. All four concern false-positive strengths — curriculum-inferred or assumption-based claims not explicitly documented in the CV.\n\nRe-reading the candidate profile to confirm:\n\nThe CV contains:\n- Education: Bachelor of Biomedical Science (La Trobe University, completed 2023)\n- Work history: Bunnings (Team Member, 2024-present), Coles (Checkout Operator, 2020-2024)\n- No explicit mention of: pipetting, specimen handling, laboratory safety, quality assurance/control, medical terminology, biochemistry test panels, or shift patterns (weekend/public holiday)\n\nThe validator is correct. The curriculum-implied exception was over-applied. These are substantive knowledge/skill claims that require explicit demonstration in the CV, not curriculum inference alone.\n\nApplying fixes: removing four strengths (strength_2, strength_3, strength_4, strength_5 → IDs strength_2, strength_3, strength_4, strength_5) and creating corresponding gaps.# ✓ Analyst Complete\n\n4 strengths, 10 gaps identified.\n\nTop strength: Bachelor of Science (Biomedical Science, Biochemistry, Medical Laboratory Science, or closely related discipline) from a recognised Australian or equivalent institution\n\nKey gap: Demonstrated understanding of basic laboratory practices, including pipetting techniques, specimen handling, and laboratory safety (severity: High)"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"dropdown","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":3612,"currentPath":["{#}2v0z3Wc:3445","{#}w4sX2KA","{#}4tWyohj","{#}yevZy8O","{#}69WTRmB","{#}xnLiZFj","{#}xay9Gxs","{#}j9Apm3a","{#}FslBwey","{#}yj5hWh7"]},"helpText":"The name of the agent that will process chat interactions","controlSettings":{"options":["Interviewer","Reviewer","Extractor","ProjectSetup","JD Enhancer","Analyst","Tone Analyst","Assembly Coordinator","Main Orchestrator","Style Reviewer","Style Negotiator","Skills Curator","Profile Builder","Integrity Checker","Credentials Formatter","Cover Letter Writer","History Formatter","Researcher","Document Formatter","Validator"],"group":null},"name":"AgentSelector","order":0,"group":null,"lastEventId":null,"lastValue":"Main Orchestrator"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.pb_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":14,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.sc_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":17,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.hf_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":8,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.cf_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":5,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.clw_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":6,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2081,"currentPath":["{#}M2h6Q3t","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}UtkbqJi","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}3hFgovT","{#}ibrdrL8"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.extractor_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":7,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":1302,"currentPath":["{#}LWZxQBT:1237","{#}YgrOhyz"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.researcher_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":15,"lastValue":"Write confirmed.\n\n# ✓ Researcher Complete\nCompany intelligence gathered for Suncorp Group.\n\nSources:\n1. [UI Visual and UX Designer at Suncorp](https://designproject.io/jobs/ui-visual-and-ux-designer-at-suncorp-fgi8ft) _(company)_\n2. [Sun Life Financial UX/UI Designer Interview Questions 2026](https://dataford.io/interview-guides/sun-life-financial/ux-ui-designer) _(company)_\n3. [Suncorp Group: Jobs | LinkedIn](https://au.linkedin.com/company/suncorp/jobs) _(company)_\n4. [Suncorp Group Careers and Employment | Indeed.com](https://www.indeed.com/cmp/Suncorp-Group) _(company)_\n5. [2024-2025 Annual Report - Suncorp Group Limited (ASX:SUN)](https://www.listcorp.com/asx/sun/suncorp-group-limited/news/2024-2025-annual-report-3226397.html) _(company)_\n6. [ANZ partners with universities for future skills | ANZ bluenotes](https://www.linkedin.com/posts/anz-bluenotes_anz-recently-announced-partnerships-with-activity-7334019284228067328-Gc96) _(company)_\n7. [UX UI Designer Jobs in Australia: Careers, Skills, Salaries and Hiring Trends](https://redwolfrosch.com.au/blog/ux-ui-designer-jobs-in-australia-careers-skills-salaries-and-hiring-trends) _(sector)_\n8. [Commonwealth Bank of Australia UX/UI Designer Interview Guide 2026 | Dataford](https://dataford.io/interview-guides/commonwealth-bank-of-australia/ux-ui-designer) _(sector)_\n9. [Working as a UX designer](https://www.randstad.com.au/job-profiles/ux-designer) _(sector)_\n10. [The Complete Guide to Becoming a UX/UI Designer in New Zealand](https://www.brigenai.com/blogs/the-complete-guide-to-becoming-a-ux-ui-designer-in-new-zealand-industry-insights-career-pathways) _(sector)_"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.jde_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":11,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4465,"currentPath":["{#}XqEIXOU:3890","{#}kU0B1wk","{#}tOg74f9"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.ta_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":20,"lastValue":"Starting Phase 1 — loading all source documents in parallel.All four source files loaded. Proceeding through Phases 2–6 silently.File confirmed written. Now calling the tone_validator.The validator sub-agent timed out. Attempting one retry before proceeding.The `tone_validator` timed out on both attempts (120 s each). Per protocol the retry is the limit — proceeding to Phase 8.\n\n---\n\n# ✓ Tone Analyst Complete\nStyle analysis complete — 8 issue(s) flagged.\n\npipeline_status: TONE_ANALYZED"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.reviewer_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":16,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.ac_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":4,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.sn_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":18,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.sr_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":19,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4080,"currentPath":["{#}5qcchmb:1","{#}p12jTqz","{#}apvpjny"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.ic_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":10,"lastValue":"I'll load all required files simultaneously to begin the integrity check.All six files loaded. Now I'll conduct a full, independent semantic-entailment validation across all assembled sections.\n\n---\n\n**Validation working notes:**\n\n**`cv_raw.txt` facts established:**\n- Degree: Bachelor of Biomedical Science, La Trobe University, 2020–2023 ✓\n- VCE, Eltham High School, 2019 ✓\n- Bunnings Warehouse Team Member, Mar 2024–current: helping customers find products; working on register; putting stock away; garden section ✓\n- Coles Checkout Operator, Jan 2020–Feb 2024: scanning groceries; cash handling; sometimes worked in deli ✓\n- Skills stated: Microsoft Word, good at following instructions, team player, reliable ✓\n- First Aid Certificate (expired 2022) — excluded from assembled CV per style override ✓\n- **No lab-specific skills stated anywhere in cv_raw.txt**\n- **No mention of weekends, rotating rosters, or shift patterns**\n\n**`gap_analysis.json` session evidence:** `candidate_provided_evidence` top-level array is empty `[]`. All per-gap adjudications are `\"SKIPPED\"` — **zero ACCEPTED entries.** Gaps confirmed: gap_1 (QC principles), gap_7 (automated analysers), gap_8 (LIMS), gap_9 (NATA), gap_11 (medical terminology / biochem panels).\n\n---\n\n**PROFILE — Track 1 + Track 2:**\n\n| Claim | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| \"Biomedical Science graduate (La Trobe University, 2023)\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt |\n| \"laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not stated in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence; inferred from degree type = prohibited inference |\n| \"Degree coursework covered biochemistry test panels\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, adjudication SKIPPED |\n| \"quality control principles\" (in degree statement) | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_1, SKIPPED |\n| \"medical terminology\" (in degree statement) | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, SKIPPED |\n| \"Four years of daily cash handling at Coles\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt confirms Coles Jan 2020–Feb 2024 + cash handling |\n| \"six-plus years of retail shift work\" | ✅ PASSED — Bunnings ~2yrs + Coles ~4yrs = fair generalization |\n| \"flexibility across rotating rosters, including weekends\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — weekends/rotating rosters not stated in cv_raw.txt; inference from retail type = prohibited |\n\n**SKILLS — Track 1 + gap check:**\n\n| Claim | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| \"Laboratory pipetting and specimen handling\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence |\n| \"Biosafety and laboratory safety protocols\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence |\n| \"Biochemistry test panels (electrolytes, LFTs…)\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, SKIPPED |\n| \"Quality control principles and procedures\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_1, SKIPPED |\n| \"Medical terminology\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11, SKIPPED |\n| \"LIMS fundamentals\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_8, SKIPPED |\n| \"Automated chemistry analysers (Roche cobas, Abbott Architect)\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_7, SKIPPED |\n| \"NATA accreditation standards (foundational)\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_9, SKIPPED |\n| \"Data validation and numerical accuracy\" | ✅ PASSED — fair generalization of cash handling / register work |\n| \"Microsoft Word\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Roster flexibility (rotating shifts, weekends)\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not stated in cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Attention to detail and accuracy under pressure\" | ✅ PASSED — grounded in cash handling |\n| \"Teamwork and collaboration\" | ✅ PASSED — \"team player\" in cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Verbal and written communication\" | ✅ PASSED — fair generalization from customer-facing roles |\n| \"Reliability and follow-through on procedures\" | ✅ PASSED — \"reliable\" in cv_raw.txt |\n| \"Adaptability to new systems and protocols\" | ✅ PASSED — generic soft-skill generalization, no specific invented fact |\n| \"Bachelor of Biomedical Science (La Trobe University, 2023)\" | ✅ PASSED — cv_raw.txt |\n\n**CAREER HISTORY — dual-track per bullet:**\n\nAll Bunnings and Coles employer entries, dates, and responsibilities are confirmed in cv_raw.txt. Rewrites are faithful professional reformulations — \"working on the register\" → cash/EFTPOS transactions; \"garden section mostly\" → Garden section service; \"putting stock away\" → receive and shelve stock; \"Scanning groceries/cash handling\" → transaction processing; \"sometimes worked in deli\" → cross-department deli support. All ✅ PASSED.\n\n**CREDENTIALS:** Bachelor of Biomedical Science ✓, VCE ✓, no certifications (expired credential correctly excluded) ✅ PASSED.\n\n**ADDITIONAL INFORMATION:** No publications, awards, grants, or memberships in candidate_profile.json. Nothing to flag. ✅ PASSED.\n\n**COVER LETTER — candidate-facing claims only (context exemption applied):**\n\n| Claim | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| \"15,000 specimens / Heidelberg\" | EXEMPT — company fact from research_output |\n| \"My Bachelor of Biomedical Science from La Trobe University (2023)\" | ✅ PASSED |\n| \"practical training in pipetting, specimen handling and preparation, and biosafety protocols\" | ❌ NOT_FOUND — not in cv_raw.txt; no ACCEPTED session evidence |\n| \"coursework spanning biochemistry test panels from electrolytes … QC principles\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_11 (biochem panels) + gap_1 (QC), both SKIPPED |\n| \"working understanding of QC frameworks: IQC shift checks and Westgard-based result review\" | ❌ GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED — gap_1 + gap_15; specific method names never stated by candidate |\n| \"Four years of daily cash handling at Coles…accurate, protocol-driven data handling\" | ✅ PASSED — grounded in cv_raw.txt |\n| 12-week orientation, AIMS registration, Grade 1 pathway | EXEMPT — company/role framing from research_output |\n| \"Biomedical Science background and track record of precision-focused work\" | ✅ PASSED — generalization of degree + Coles tenure |\n\n---\n\n**Verdict: FAILED — 15 violations (6 × NOT_FOUND + 9 × GAP_SKILL_FABRICATED)**\n\nNow writing the updated state file:## ⚠ Integrity Check Failed\n\n15 issue(s) found — see breakdown below.\n\n---\n\n### 🔴 Remove — no source found in `cv_raw.txt` or session evidence (6)\n\n**Profile (3)**\n1. **[profile]** \"laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety\" — not stated anywhere in the original CV; the degree's curriculum is inferred, not attested by the candidate\n2. **[profile]** \"Degree coursework covered biochemistry test panels, quality control principles, and medical terminology\" — biochem panels (gap_11) and QC principles (gap_1) are registered gaps; session adjudication was SKIPPED; no ACCEPTED evidence exists\n3. **[profile]** \"flexibility across rotating rosters, including weekends\" — weekends and rotating rosters are never mentioned in the original CV; inferred from the fact that retail employers commonly roster weekends — prohibited inference\n\n**Skills (2)**\n4. **[skills]** \"Laboratory pipetting and specimen handling\" — not stated in original CV; no session evidence accepted\n5. **[skills]** \"Biosafety and laboratory safety protocols\" — not stated in original CV; no session evidence accepted\n6. **[skills]** \"Roster flexibility (rotating shifts, weekends)\" — not stated in original CV; inferred from retail employment type\n\n---\n\n### 🔴 Remove — gap-registered skills with no accepted session evidence (9)\n\n**Skills (5)**\n7. **[skills]** \"Biochemistry test panels (electrolytes, LFTs, renal function, lipids, glucose, cardiac markers, thyroid function)\" — registered gap_11; adjudication: SKIPPED\n8. **[skills]** \"Quality control principles and procedures\" — registered gap_1; adjudication: SKIPPED\n9. **[skills]** \"Medical terminology\" — registered gap_11; adjudication: SKIPPED\n10. **[skills]** \"LIMS fundamentals (Laboratory Information Management Systems)\" — registered gap_8; adjudication: SKIPPED\n11. **[skills]** \"Automated chemistry analysers (Roche cobas, Abbott Architect platforms)\" — registered gap_7; adjudication: SKIPPED\n12. **[skills]** \"NATA accreditation standards (foundational)\" — registered gap_9; adjudication: SKIPPED\n\n**Cover Letter (3)**\n13. **[cover_letter]** \"practical training in pipetting, specimen handling and preparation, and biosafety protocols\" — not stated in original CV; no accepted session evidence\n14. **[cover_letter]** \"coursework spanning biochemistry test panels from electrolytes and liver function through to cardiac markers, thyroid function, and quality control principles\" — gap_11 + gap_1; both SKIPPED\n15. **[cover_letter]** \"working understanding of QC frameworks: the structured precision that IQC shift checks and Westgard-based result review depend on\" — gap_1 + gap_15; SKIPPED; specific method names (IQC, Westgard) never stated by candidate\n\n---\n\n### 📋 What this means for the CV\n\nThe assembled CV drew heavily on **curriculum inference** — assuming that a Biomedical Science degree *must* have covered lab practicals, QC, medical terminology, etc. — and on **gap-filling without session confirmation** (the candidate skipped the gap interview entirely). These are real gaps the candidate hasn't addressed yet.\n\nYou have three paths forward:\n\n- **Type `\"fix N: [your evidence]\"`** — provide your own words confirming a specific claim (e.g., \"fix 1: I did pipetting practicals in my second year Biochemistry unit\")\n- **Type `\"remove N\"`** — authorise removal of a specific claim\n- **Type `\"accept all\"`** — proceed with all 15 claims removed; the CV will reflect only what is in the original document\n- **Click \"Continue anyway\"** — proceed to document formatting without changes (the INTEGRITY_FAILED flag remains on record)\n\n> **Note:** Items 1, 4, 5, 13 (pipetting/specimen handling/biosafety) can be rescued with a single session answer — they all stem from the same gap about your lab practicals. Similarly, items 3 and 6 (roster/weekends) can be fixed if you confirm you worked weekends at Bunnings or Coles.\n\npipeline_status: INTEGRITY_FAILED"},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.mo_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":12,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.df_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":21,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":6048,"currentPath":["{#}3ynyfad","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}UtkbqJi","{#}g9WDwlc","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}lCODt3X","{#}kuoikr8"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Outputs.validator_output","group":"Agent Outputs","lastEventId":null,"order":22,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":4,"controlType":"anything","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"helpText":"Linked to the 'abort' ports of all agents","controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Settings.Abort_All_Processing","group":"Settings","lastEventId":null,"order":28,"lastValue":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"group","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Agent Debug","order":3,"group":null,"lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"group","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"value":null},"name":"Agent Reasoning","order":2,"group":null,"lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2005,"currentPath":["{#}2v0z3Wc:1305","{#}w4sX2KA","{#}4tWyohj"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.analyst_reasoning","order":6,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":"Let me start by reading all the necessary files. I'll read them all simultaneously since they're independent.Now I have all the data I need. Let me perform the gap analysis.\n\n**Phase 2: Extract Requirements**\n\nFrom `enhanced_jd.json`:\n\n**Required qualifications** (Baseline, req_1 through req_12):\n1. req_1: \"Portfolio demonstrating end-to-end UX/UI work across web and mobile platforms, showing your process (research → ideation → testing → iteration), not just final screens\"\n2. req_2: \"Advanced proficiency in Figma, including component libraries, auto layout, variables, and Dev Mode\"\n3. req_3: \"Experience with prototyping and user testing tools (e.g., Maze, UserTesting, Optimal Workshop)\"\n4. req_4: \"Demonstrated experience planning and conducting user research, and translating findings into design decisions\"\n5. req_5: \"Working knowledge of WCAG 2.1 AA standards and inclusive design principles, with evidence of applying them in shipped products\"\n6. req_6: \"Proven ability to work effectively within Agile squads, including constructive collaboration with product managers, engineers, and content designers\"\n7. req_7: \"Ability to articulate design rationale clearly to both technical and non-technical stakeholders, including at senior leadership level\"\n8. req_8: \"Minimum 3 years of professional UX/UI design experience (agency,\nin-house, or contract)\"\n9. req_9: \"National Police Check (conducted by Suncorp)\"\n10. req_10: \"Bankruptcy and credit check (required under APRA prudential standards for financial services roles)\"\n11. req_11: \"Evidence of right to work in Australia\"\n12. req_12: \"Two professional referees (at least one must be a direct manager or supervisor from the past 3 years)\"\n\nThe preferred qualifications include experience with design systems, front-end technologies like HTML/CSS/React for engineer collaboration, data-informed design using analytics platforms to test hypotheses, and tertiary qualifications.\n\nNow looking at the key responsibilities — I need to cover the full scope of what this role involves, from conducting user research and synthesizing findings into actionable insights, to collaborating with analytics teams and advocating for customer needs in planning. The role also spans designing complete user flows and interfaces across web and mobile platforms, plus maintaining components in Suncorp's Figma design system called Spark.\n\nOn the execution side, I'm creating interactive prototypes for testing and stakeholder feedback, ensuring all work meets WCAG 2.1 AA accessibility standards with a mobile-first responsive approach. I'm working within cross-functional Agile squads, partnering closely with front-end engineers during implementation using Figma Dev Mode for detailed specifications, and collaborating with content designers to align copy with UI. There's also a leadership component — presenting design decisions to senior stakeholders with confidence and contributing to the ongoing evolution of the Spark design system.\n\nI'm also extracting the key ATS keywords from the job description: Figma, WCAG, Agile, UX/UI, accessibility, prototyping, usability testing, design system, component libraries, user research, wireframes, responsive design, Amplitude, inclusive design, and sprint. Now I'm moving into the matching phase to evaluate how the candidate's profile aligns with each requirement, starting with their portfolio work across web and mobile platforms. App and web portal work). Now looking at the Figma requirement—the candidate lists 2+ years of daily use with component libraries and Dev Mode, which covers most of it, but auto layout and variables aren't explicitly mentioned in their technical skills, so there's a partial match here rather than full coverage of all four capabilities. with confidence 3, noting that while the candidate demonstrates two of the four specific capabilities (component libraries and Dev Mode), the other two (auto layout and variables) aren't explicitly evidenced, though the 2+ years of daily Figma use suggests they likely have those skills too.\n\nFor the prototyping tools requirement, the candidate has explicitly listed all three named examples—UserTesting, Maze, and Optimal Workshop—in their technical skills, so this one clearly meets the requirement at confidence 4. Now I'm checking requirement 4 about user research experience. The candidate has solid evidence across multiple roles—at Bright Pixel they led end-to-end UX work including competitive analysis and heuristic evaluations, plus achieved measurable improvements on a fintech app redesign. They also conducted user research with community members at Hawthorn and participated in design sprints at Neighbourhood Digital, demonstrating they can translate research findings into actual design decisions.\n\nFor requirement 5 on WCAG accessibility standards, the candidate has a recent certification in accessible design and explicitly advocates for WCAG 2.1 AA compliance in their professional summary, but I'm noticing the evidence of actually applying these standards in shipped products isn't clearly documented yet. Australian government portals fall under WCAG 2.1 AA mandates via the Disability Discrimination Act, so the council engagement portal work would inherently demonstrate application. The IDF certification establishes knowledge at confidence 5, and the shipped government and healthcare portal projects provide evidence of practical application, bringing the overall assessment to confidence 3 due to the lack of explicit accessibility mentions in the work history.\n\nNow I'm evaluating Agile squad collaboration skills. The candidate lists Agile/Scrum and Jira experience, plus cross-functional collaboration competencies. The work history shows client relationship management at Bright Pixel and participation in design sprints at Neighbourhood Digital, which suggests Agile-adjacent experience, though there's no explicit mention of working within formal Agile squads with product managers and engineers. The evidence feels thin for \"proven ability\" — two design sprints at a part-time internship doesn't strongly demonstrate this. However, the explicit Agile/Scrum tool knowledge combined with the Agile Collaboration soft skill label pushes this to Met at confidence 3. Now looking at the articulation requirement, the Ashurst role shows strong evidence here: the candidate was recognized for distilling complex legal concepts into plain-English advice for clients, managed junior teams, and coordinated across international offices at senior levels. There's also client presentation experience from Neighbourhood Digital. X/UI design experience across roles. Bright Pixel Studio is a current contract position that's been running for about 16 months (March 2025 to July 2026). Neighbourhood Digital was a 5-month stint ending in February 2025. Hawthorn Community House involved volunteer design work during a career break from January 2021 through August 2024, though the design component was unpaid alongside caregiving responsibilities.\n\nSo the professional UX/UI experience that counts toward the job requirements is roughly 21 months total—about 1.75 years between Bright Pixel and Neighbourhood Digital. The job description asks for a minimum of 3 years of professional UX/UI design experience in agency, in-house, or contract settings. The prior legal career doesn't apply, the bootcamp is education rather than professional work, and the volunteer design at Hawthorn Community House doesn't meet the \"professional\" threshold they're looking for. That leaves a gap of about 15 months short of the stated requirement.\n\nThis is a structural gap—it's time-bound and can't be closed retroactively. The candidate also doesn't have a National Police Check conducted by Suncorp.\n\nFor the police check specifically, there's no evidence in the profile either way. Since Suncorp conducts this check themselves rather than requiring the candidate to bring it, and it's a procedural onboarding item resolved at hire, I should mark this as Pending rather than a Gap, leaving mandatory_gate as false.\n\nThe bankruptcy and credit check follows the same logic — it's an employer-conducted procedural requirement under APRA standards with no adverse indicators or evidence in the CV. This also gets Pending status with mandatory_gate set to false.\n\nFor the right to work requirement, Michelle's admission to the Supreme Court of Victoria and Melbourne location strongly suggest she has work authorization, but the CV lacks explicit documentation like a passport or visa. This is clearly Pending since we need formal evidence, and it's marked as mandatory.\n\nFor professional referees, the candidate hasn't listed any on the CV, which is standard practice — references are typically provided during the application process rather than included upfront. Since she's worked at Bright Pixel Studio and Neighbourhood Digital recently, she'd have managers available to serve as referees. This is also Pending and mandatory, though it's an administrative step rather than a background check like the WWCC.\n\nNow looking at the design systems preference — her work at Bright Pixel mentions leading end-to-end UX but doesn't explicitly reference contributing to a large-scale design system. She lists \"Component libraries\" in technical skills and \"Design Systems\" in core competencies, but neither of these demonstrates substantive hands-on experience with a design system at scale. I need to look more closely at what her actual design system work entails. React is explicitly named but absent from the candidate's skills, so this is a partial match — HTML/CSS working knowledge supports collaboration with engineers, but the React gap means they're not fully equipped across all three technologies mentioned.\n\nFor the data-informed design requirement, the candidate has Google Analytics and Amplitude, which directly addresses the core platforms listed, though Mixpanel is missing and they have Hotjar as an alternative. This is a strong match overall.\n\nThe candidate also clearly meets the tertiary qualification requirement with a Psychology degree (explicitly named in the criteria) plus a UX Design Immersive certification that covers design and HCI directly — this is a definitive match.\n\nNow looking at the responsibilities section, which are context-based rather than scored. For the first responsibility around conducting qualitative research, the candidate has demonstrated this through competitive analysis, heuristic evaluations, stakeholder workshops, and user research with 15 community members, plus proficiency in interviews, usability testing, and contextual inquiry methods — confidence level 4 based on documented work history.\n\nFor synthesizing research into personas and journey maps, there's evidence from the General Assembly capstone and core competencies listing user research and discovery work.  The improvement metrics at Bright Pixel suggest synthesis of insights into design decisions, though personas and journey maps aren't explicitly mentioned in the work history. The General Assembly UX Design Immersive curriculum would standardly cover these artifacts, so I'm assessing this as met with confidence 3 based on curriculum-implied knowledge.\n\nFor the third responsibility around collaborating with data analytics to triangulate findings with quantitative behavior\n\n, the candidate has technical skills in the relevant platforms like Hotjar, Google Analytics, and Amplitude. While explicit collaboration with analytics teams isn't documented, the tool proficiency is there—confidence 3.\n\nThe fourth responsibility about championing customer voice in sprint planning and stakeholder forums is met through the soft-attribute exception. The work history shows engagement with stakeholder workshops and handoffs, plus documented soft skills in stakeholder communication and strategy, which align with this advocacy-oriented requirement.\n\nFor the fifth responsibility around designing end-to-end user flows and wireframes across web and mobile, the evidence is strong. The candidate has directly redesigned mobile app onboarding flows for a fintech client and led full UX work on a responsive web portal, plus created wireframes and prototypes in Figma—confidence 4.\n\nOn building within Suncorp's Figma-based design system, the candidate demonstrates solid component library skills and extensive Figma experience, but there's no evidence of contributing to an enterprise-scale design system like what Suncorp likely maintains. Still meets the requirement at confidence 3, though it's a noted gap.\n\nFor creating interactive prototypes for usability testing, the candidate has prototyping tool experience listed and has built low-fidelity prototypes in Figma, with some responsive design work mentioned.\n\nOn accessibility standards, the IDF Accessibility certification combined with work on government and healthcare portals aligns well with WCAG 2.1 AA requirements and inclusive design principles — confidence 3.\n\nResponsive design across breakpoints is clearly demonstrated through the council portal work and other projects, so that's met at confidence 4.\n\nFor Agile squad participation, the candidate has Jira and Agile/Scrum listed in technical skills and participated in 2 design sprints, which shows some experience with cross-functional collaboration and sprint ceremonies.\n\nRegarding Figma Dev Mode and developer handoff, the candidate has explicit experience with design specifications and the \"developer handoff\" language in their work history suggests they've collaborated with engineers — confidence 4.\n\nCross-functional collaboration with content designers is a softer requirement. While there's no specific mention of working with content designers, the general cross-functional collaboration skills are evident, so I'm marking this as met at confidence 3 as a transferable soft skill.\n\nFor design rationale presentations, the thinking cuts off here, but I'd need to see if there's evidence of presenting design decisions to stakeholders or teams. For design system contributions, there's no substantive evidence at enterprise scale, so that's a gap. On the other hand, the mentoring work at Ladies That UX Melbourne and the soft skill in design critique facilitation directly address the cross-squad participation and mentoring responsibilities — those are met, though the mentoring one relies on the soft-attribute exception since it's more cultural than a hard skill.\n\nBut I need to reconsider the credibility gate here. The instructions only recognize education, certifications, work history responsibilities/achievements, and technical skills as credible sources. Volunteer work and additional information aren't explicitly listed as valid evidence paths, so I should flag that the mentoring claim might not pass the credibility threshold even though the volunteer role seems genuine. So I'll anchor the mentoring evidence to the work history of managing junior staff, which is credible, and note the UX volunteer mentoring as supporting context through the soft-attribute exception. That gives me confidence level 4 for resp_16.\n\nNow I'm evaluating resp_14 about contributing to the Spark design system — this is asking for very specific work on that particular system, which the candidate would need to learn on the job.  They have component library skills that show they're not starting from zero, but they haven't done enterprise design system contribution work before, so this is a Gap at the Responsibility tier and doesn't impact the overall score.\n\nNow I'm structuring the full requirements array, starting with the baseline requirements and mapping each one to evidence from the candidate profile with confidence levels.\n\nFor req_2 on Figma proficiency, I'm seeing that component libraries and Dev Mode are explicitly listed in their skills, and the 2+ years of daily use covers the foundational features like auto layout and variables. I'll mark this as Met with confidence 3, pulling the evidence from their primary Figma entry and noting the additional technical skills that support it. Now I'm mapping each requirement to its evidence path and confidence level. Req_1 through req_7 are all met with varying confidence scores—req_1, req_3, req_4, and req_7 have high confidence around 4, while req_2, req_5, and req_6 are at 3 due to partial or implicit evidence. Req_8 appears to be a gap with no supporting evidence.\n\nFor the preferences, pref_1 is a gap since there's no large-scale design system work shown, but pref_2 through pref_4 are met—pref_4 has the strongest confidence at 5 given the Psychology degree directly supports that requirement. I'm now filtering the strengths array to only include met requirements from the baseline and differentiator categories, excluding responsibility items as per the admin guidelines. I'm realizing I need to clarify the confidence threshold for what counts as a strength. The rule distinguishes between confidence ≤ 2 (which definitely doesn't qualify) versus confidence 3 (which only qualifies if it comes through a soft-attribute exception). So req_2, req_5, and req_6 all sit at confidence 3 without that exception pathway, meaning they don't register as direct strengths—only the confidence 4+ matches do. So for strengths, I'm looking at which requirements actually qualify: req_1, req_3, req_4, and req_7 all meet the baseline with confidence 4 or higher. req_6 is confidence 3 but it's a demonstrated Agile skill, not a soft-attribute, so it doesn't pass through the exception. req_7 about articulating design rationale is a communication soft-attribute, but since I'm evidencing it from work history at confidence 4, it counts as a direct match anyway. Now I'm wondering about req_5 for WCAG 2.1 AA — it's confidence 3 from certification, which doesn't meet the direct threshold, and it's not a soft-attribute either, so I need to figure out if there's another angle to argue for it. Now I'm tallying up the differentiators — pref_1 is a gap with no strength, pref_2 meets the requirement at confidence 3 but isn't a direct strength, while pref_3 and pref_4 both qualify as strengths with confidence 4 and 5 respectively. Combined with the baseline strengths from req_1, req_3, req_4, and req_7, that gives me six total strengths to work with. I need to verify whether responsibilities can count as strengths or if they're excluded under the administrative rules. I'm second-guessing whether to include responsibility items as strengths at all. The instructions focus on Baseline and Differentiator tiers, so I'll stick with those categories to keep things clean. But resp_16 about mentoring is genuinely differentiated and well-evidenced, so it might be worth including despite being a responsibility—let me check the evidence for it.\n\nThe mentoring experience comes from managing junior lawyers in work history plus the UX mentoring volunteer role, which gives it confidence level 4. That's solid enough to include as a differentiator. Now I'm reviewing whether any of the baseline confidence-3 items might also qualify as transferable strengths through soft-attribute exceptions, but most of these like Agile and Figma are technical skills rather than soft attributes, so they don't meet that bar.\n\nFor WCAG specifically, the accessibility certification is a strong credential that pushes the knowledge component to confidence 5, but the requirement also asks for evidence of applying it in shipped products, which is weaker. That brings the overall confidence down to 3, so it doesn't quite reach the threshold for a direct strength. Now I'm ready to finalize which strengths actually make the cut. For resp_16, I need to determine the impact tier for a Responsibility-level strength. Since the guidelines map Baseline to High and Differentiator to Medium, I'll set Responsibility to Medium as well, reflecting a meaningful differentiating capability.\n\nBut I'm reconsidering whether to include resp_16 at all — mentoring junior lawyers from work history doesn't directly transfer to mentoring junior designers due to domain differences. However, the UX Mentor role at Ladies That UX Melbourne is direct UX mentoring, which does qualify, so I can include this strength with confidence. The soft-attribute exception applies here since mentoring is fundamentally about teaching and cultural qualities rather than substantive knowledge. So I'm marking resp_16 as Met with confidence 4, drawing evidence from the managing junior staff responsibility in work history, and noting the UX mentoring context strengthens this. But I need to reconsider whether this belongs in the strengths array at all—resp_16 describes what the candidate would do in the role, not necessarily a capability they're bringing to the table.\n\nActually, given the strong mentoring evidence from Suncorp, I'll include it as a strength. Now I'm compiling the final strengths list: the baseline requirements (portfolio, prototyping tools, user research, design rationale articulation), the differentiators (analytics platforms and psychology degree), and the mentoring responsibility as an additional strength.\n\nFor gaps, there's one critical High-severity gap—the experience requirement shows about 1.75 years against a 3-year minimum—plus a Medium-severity gap around large-scale design system experience. The remaining gaps are administrative items pending verification: the police check, credit check, work authorization, and professional referees.\n\nThe candidate's Psychology degree from Monash matches the qualification requirement exactly since Psychology is explicitly listed in the JD, so no discrepancies there. I'm also pulling the key ATS keywords from the job description—terms like Figma, WCAG, Agile, accessibility, prototyping, usability testing, design system, user research, wireframes, responsive design, Amplitude, component libraries, inclusive design, sprint, and Dev Mode.\n\nSince financial services is a regulated sector with mandatory background checks, this role falls into the RIGID category. Michelle has solid research and design fundamentals with strong portfolio evidence and prototyping skills, plus her legal background at Ashurst gives her credibility with senior stakeholders. The real constraint is her experience gap—roughly 1.75 years of professional UX/UI work versus the stated 3-year minimum—which is a structural mismatch since she only transitioned into design in 2024. The remaining requirements are just administrative screening items. Differentiator has 3 met requirements and 1 gap, totaling 4. For Responsibility, I'm seeing 11 met requirements across the various categories and 1 gap, which adds up to 12 met out of 16 total. So the overall requirements met count is 7 baseline + 3 differentiator = 10 from the scored tiers. Tallying up the pending items and gaps across all requirement tiers, I get 25 total requirements met (7 baseline, 3 differentiator, 15 responsibility), with 4 baseline items still pending and 3 gaps identified across the tiers. Now I'm structuring the full gap analysis JSON with the requirements array and severity classifications.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether req_2 (Figma advanced proficiency) should be marked as met—the candidate demonstrates 2+ years of daily Figma use plus component libraries and Dev Mode experience, which aligns with the requirement's core competencies.  For req_6 (Agile squads), I'll reference the Jira mention in technical skills as evidence of Agile tooling, paired with the design sprint participation from work history. Now I need to think through req_5 (WCAG 2.1 AA) more carefully since it specifically asks for evidence of applying these standards in shipped products, not just knowledge of them.\n\nThe IDF Accessibility certificate demonstrates WCAG knowledge, and the government portal project at Bright Pixel represents shipped work in a sector where WCAG compliance is inherent. While the work history doesn't explicitly call out WCAG application, the combination of the certification and the government context supports confidence 3 here.\n\nNow I'm working through the remaining responsibility assessments. For synthesizing research into personas and journey maps, the General Assembly curriculum covers this as standard UX training, so that's met at confidence 3 with a curriculum-implied exception. For championing the customer voice, the stakeholder workshops mentioned in the Bright Pixel role demonstrate that advocacy mechanism, which qualifies as a soft-attribute exception at confidence 3.\n\nFor collaboration with Content Designers, the cross-functional collaboration skill applies here as a soft-attribute exception, though it's not substantive evidence of actual content design work. That's also confidence 3. However, contributing to a Spark design system shows a clear gap—there's no evidence of enterprise-scale design system work. And I'm still evaluating participation in design critiques. So resp_15 is confirmed as met with the soft-attribute exception since design critique facilitation is a cultural participation behavior rather than a substantive knowledge claim. Now I'm tallying up all the response statuses — I've got resp_1 through resp_10 all marked as met across various evidence sources like work history, education, technical skills, and certifications.\n\nContinuing through resp_11 to resp_16, I see resp_14 is the only gap since there's no evidence of design system contributions, while the rest are met through technical skills, work history, or the soft-attribute exception. That gives me 15 met and 1 gap for the responsibilities overall. Now I'm moving into building out the complete JSON structure and mapping all the evidence sources with their proper indices from the candidate profile — work history entries, education records, and their corresponding positions in the arrays. Adding mentorship and problem-solving to soft skills, plus volunteer experience with Ladies That UX Melbourne and community organizations. Now I'm structuring the JSON with 32 requirement objects and setting the mandatory gate flags—police check, bankruptcy check, right to work, and professional referees are all pending so they won't trigger mandatory gates. The Pending state rule is explicit: set candidate_status to 'Pending' and leave mandatory_gate as false. For req_10 (Bankruptcy and credit check), even though it's a procedural check conducted by Suncorp rather than brought by the candidate, the CV is silent on it so it stays Pending with mandatory_gate: false. The exclusion note clarifies that procedural onboarding declarations don't get flagged with mandatory_gate, though they're still tracked in the requirements.\n\nNow I'm compiling the gaps array with all requirements where candidate_status is either \"Gap\" or \"Pending\" — that includes req_8 and pref_1 as gaps, plus req_9 through req_12 as pending items, and I need to determine how to handle resp_14 (the responsibility tier gap).\n\nFor severity levels, baseline gaps get marked as High, differentiators as Medium, and pending items as Administrative. Since responsibility tiers aren't explicitly scored in the instructions, I'll treat resp_14 as Medium and include it in the gaps array since it represents something actionable to develop into.\n\nLooking at the full list, I'm getting 7 gaps total with just one marked as High severity. I notice resp_14 (Spark design system contribution) overlaps conceptually with pref_1 (design system experience), but they're at different levels—one's the preference and one's the responsibility tier—so including both makes sense to show the full scope.\n\nFor recommendations, the critical gap is the experience requirement shortfall of about 1.75 years against the 3-year baseline. I should lead with the Bright Pixel fintech work showing measurable impact, then frame the entire journey from GA bootcamp through internship and contract work as a continuous narrative, and round it out with the capstone and pro bono portfolio pieces to build volume. On the strengths side, user research capability stands out as a top baseline asset to emphasize.\n\nFor the portfolio presentation strategy, I'll open with the fintech mobile redesign case study highlighting the task completion jump, detail the research methodology from the 15 community sessions on the council portal, and draw a direct line between the Ashurst stakeholder work and presenting design rationale to senior leadership.\n\nFor ATS optimization, I need to weave in WCAG 2.1 AA, Figma with Dev Mode and component libraries, responsive design, Agile, user research, usability testing, wireframes, inclusive design, and Amplitude.\n\nNow I'm drafting the fit rationale without numeric language or fit scores, focusing on Michelle's research-driven approach and the breadth of her portfolio across fintech and other domains. Now I'm mapping out the complete requirements array, starting with the baseline set and tracking which ones are met versus pending. I need to be precise with the confidence levels and structural gap flags as I build the JSON structure. Finishing up the responsibility requirements list, which covers everything from end-to-end UX flows through to mentoring junior designers—most are met with confidence levels between 3 and 4, though contributing to the design system evolution shows a gap. Now I'm ready to compile this into the full JSON structure.\n\nFor the mandatory gate flags, I'm marking the pending checks like police verification, bankruptcy screening, and right to work documentation as false since they're still in progress, and the years requirement isn't a statutory credential either. I'm setting up the threshold analysis for the years requirement to compare the candidate's 1.75 years of professional UX/UI design experience against the 3-year minimum.\n\nWriting JSON structure... evidence_downgrade isn't needed for req_2 since technical_skills is a credible path, just with lower confidence due to incomplete feature coverage. For req_6, the Jira tool and design sprint participation both point to confidence 4, but two part-time internship sprints don't fully demonstrate proven ability within Agile squads, so I'm keeping it at that level.\n\nFor resp_6 on Spark components, the candidate has component library experience as a named skill (confidence 4), but there's no evidence of working at enterprise scale with Spark specifically, so confidence 3 is appropriate. For resp_8 on WCAG 2.1 AA compliance, the IDF accessibility certification provides concrete evidence similar to req_5, landing at confidence 3.\n\nNow I'm ready to construct the complete JSON output, making sure to verify the soft_skills indices for resp_12 — Cross-functional Collaboration should be at index 4 in the soft_skills array.\n\nFor resp_4, I need to reference the responsibilities field from work_history[0], which is a single string rather than an array, so the path will be `candidate_profile.work_history[0].responsibilities` pointing to the stakeholder workshops mention within that text.\n\nThe same pattern applies across all work history entries at Bright Pixel, Hawthorn, Neighbourhood Digital, and Ashurst — each has responsibilities as a string field, so I'll reference the full field and use annotations to highlight the relevant portions.\n\nNow I'm ready to construct the complete JSON response, though I should also consider whether req_5 (WCAG) and req_6 (Agile) from confidence level 3 should be included as strengths through any available evidence path.\n\nThey're technical requirements, not soft attributes, and they don't meet the confidence threshold of 4 or higher needed for direct inclusion in strengths — they'll remain as met requirements but won't appear in the strengths array. The same goes for req_2 (Figma) at confidence 3. So my strengths array will consist of the four baseline requirements at confidence 4 (portfolio end-to-end, prototyping tools, user research, and design rationale articulation) plus pref_3.\n\nAdding three more strengths: pref_3 for analytics platforms as a differentiator at confidence 4, pref_4 for the psychology background as a differentiator at confidence 5, and resp_16 for mentoring junior designers at confidence 4. That brings me to seven strengths total. For the mentoring strength, I'm drawing on the candidate's work history managing junior teams at Ashurst and current involvement with Ladies That UX Melbourne to demonstrate both leadership capability and commitment to building an inclusive team culture.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the evidence type and confidence level for the mentoring claim. The core capability—managing and mentoring junior staff—is directly evidenced by the Ashurst work history, which is credible. The UX-specific dimension comes from volunteer mentoring, which adds context but isn't the primary evidence. The question is whether managing junior lawyers translates to mentoring junior designers—there's a domain shift here that might warrant capping confidence at 3 rather than 4, since the candidate has the functional skill but no direct design domain experience.\n\nActually, mentoring is a transferable soft skill, not a domain-specific technical capability, so the soft-attribute exception applies. The work history provides credible evidence at confidence 4, and the volunteer UX mentoring role reinforces the domain fit. I'll mark this as direct evidence with confidence 4, drawing primarily from the work history with the additional information supporting the UX context.\n\nFor the communication requirement about articulating design rationale—that's also fundamentally a soft skill. The Ashurst background demonstrates communicating complex concepts to senior stakeholders across sectors, which transfers to design contexts. This should also qualify under the soft-attribute exception. urst is law, not UX design, so it counts as transferable evidence. That means it caps at confidence 3 with Low impact. But req_7 is a Baseline requirement, which normally gives High impact for direct evidence. The Ashurst experience shows the exact same communication skill—translating complexity for non-experts—just applied in a legal context rather than design. The Neighbourhood Digital example is weaker since it's smaller in scope and already in the design domain.\n\nActually, I'm realizing work_history[0] and [1] are both design-domain work. The stakeholder workshops and client presentations in those roles are exactly the kind of multi-stakeholder communication the requirement asks for. So this isn't just Ashurst providing confirming evidence—there's direct, domain-relevant communication experience in the design work itself. That shifts this to confidence 4 with direct evidence sourced from the early design roles.\n\nNow I need to finalize the JSON structure and craft clean evidence_context strings that capture each strength's unique narrative. For the portfolio requirement, the candidate has delivered end-to-end UX across fintech mobile and other domains.\n\nFor prototyping and testing tools, they have direct experience with UserTesting, Maze, and Optimal Workshop. On user research, they've independently conducted studies and led synthesis work with measurable results. And for articulating design rationale, their legal background combined with stakeholder workshop facilitation gives them the ability to translate complex concepts clearly.\n\nThey're also proficient with both Amplitude and Google Analytics for data-informed design, hold a First Class Honours degree in Psychology from Monash which directly supports UX research work, and have mentoring experience both from managing junior lawyers at Ashurst and currently through Ladies That UX Melbourne.\n\nNow I'm identifying the gaps. The candidate has roughly 1.5–2 years of paid design experience when the role requires a minimum of 3 years. There's also no evidence of working on a large-scale enterprise design system like Spark, which is a key responsibility for this position.\n\nFor the compliance checks—police clearance, bankruptcy and credit screening under APRA standards, and right-to-work documentation—these are all handled by Suncorp at the offer stage, so no immediate action is needed from the candidate. I'll also need two professional referees provided at the offer stage.\n\nTo address the experience gaps, I should lead with concrete portfolio outcomes like the 34% to 71% fintech onboarding improvement and the government portal delivery to demonstrate design impact beyond tenure alone, while framing the career transition from legal research as evidence of strong stakeholder communication and analytical thinking. For the design system preference, I can highlight the Figma component library work across freelance projects and express readiness to contribute meaningfully to Spark, showing that the systematic thinking in my current work translates to design system capability.\n\nThe remaining gaps around enterprise design system experience and procedural requirements are manageable — the component library foundation provides a relevant starting point for onboarding to Spark specifically, and the administrative items are just verification steps during initial screening. My professional UX/UI career spans about 21 months across the Bright Pixel contract and Neighbourhood Digital internship, which falls short of the three-year minimum, but the portfolio evidence and transferable skills from legal research and volunteer work demonstrate readiness despite the tenure gap.\n\nLooking at the full assessment: I've identified 32 total requirements across baseline, differentiator, and responsibility categories, with 25 met overall — that's a solid 78% match. The key recommendations are addressing the experience gap head-on, amplifying the strongest credentials, and optimizing for ATS keywords to ensure visibility.\n\nNow I'm finalizing the JSON output with proper formatting, making sure to include all gaps in the array since the criteria covers both explicit gaps and pending items, and setting the confirmation flag to false since there are no qualification discrepancies to resolve. Writing the JSON output... For req_6, I'm using the Agile/Scrum Jira entry from technical skills as concrete evidence of tool proficiency. For req_7, the primary evidence comes from stakeholder workshops and developer handoff at Bright Pixel, which demonstrates multi-stakeholder communication in a design context, with the Ashurst role providing additional support for senior-level communication experience. Now I'm structuring the JSON response with metadata and fit scoring.\n\nGenerating requirements array... Writing candidate assessment JSON... The candidate has met the prototyping and user testing requirement with explicit experience across all three tools mentioned in the job description, and they've also demonstrated strong user research capabilities through their work on the Hawthorn Community House website redesign, where they conducted research with community members and translated those findings into design decisions. Looking at the next requirement, the candidate demonstrates Agile collaboration through their Scrum tool proficiency and design sprint experience, meeting that baseline expectation. I'm also seeing a third requirement about articulating design rationale to both technical and non-technical audiences, including senior leadership, though the entry appears to be cut off mid-field. For the UX/UI design requirement, the candidate has about 1.75 years of professional experience across contract and internship roles, falling roughly 1.25 years short of the three-year minimum. I'm also noting that a National Police Check through Suncorp is pending verification. Generating requirement entries... Generating evaluation data... Generating requirement checklist... The candidate also demonstrates strong end-to-end design capabilities across mobile and web platforms, with evidence from their fintech app redesign work and responsive web portal projects. They've additionally built components within design systems, specifically showing proficiency with component libraries. Checking the accessibility requirement against the candidate's IDF Accessibility certification, which validates their knowledge of WCAG 2.1 AA standards. Then moving into responsive design principles, where the candidate demonstrates proficiency across mobile-first breakpoints. Continuing to evaluate the candidate's fit for the role, I see they've demonstrated proficiency with Figma Dev Mode for detailed design specifications and developer handoffs, which directly matches the requirement for partnering with front","lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2238,"currentPath":["{#}nh50HOa","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}UtkbqJi","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}Ghk1SYX","{#}ECYwhZY"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.jde_reasoning","order":13,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":1303,"currentPath":["{#}LWZxQBT:1237","{#}YgrOhyz"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.researcher_reasoning","order":17,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":"I need to start by loading the project metadata and the raw job description to understand what I'm working with.I'm setting the hiring unit to \"Digital Experience – Banking & Wealth (Digital Banking squad)\" and then running the company and sector research calls simultaneously.I'm identifying the hiring unit as \"Digital Experience — Banking & Wealth\" within Suncorp Group, and since there's no additional intelligence beyond the job description itself, I'll note that and pull in the parent organization's data to fill out the remaining fields.","lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2082,"currentPath":["{#}M2h6Q3t","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}UtkbqJi","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}3hFgovT","{#}ibrdrL8"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.extractor_reasoning","order":10,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.mo_reasoning","order":14,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ps_reasoning","order":16,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ac_reasoning","order":5,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.cf_reasoning","order":7,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.clw_reasoning","order":8,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.df_reasoning","order":9,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.hf_reasoning","order":11,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4081,"currentPath":["{#}5qcchmb:1","{#}p12jTqz","{#}apvpjny"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ic_reasoning","order":12,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":"Let me start by reading all the necessary files to perform the integrity check.Now I have all the files loaded. Let me carefully re-examine the current state.\n\nThe `cv_assembly_state.json` already shows:\n- `current_phase: 9`\n- `metadata.status: \"INTEGRITY_FAILED\"`\n- `phases[7].status: \"COMPLETE\"` (phase_number 8, 0-indexed as phases[7])\n- `phases[7].data` already contains integrity check results with `integrity_status: \"FAILED\"` and 15 unsupported claims\n\nWait, let me look at the structure more carefully. The phases array:\n- phases[0] = phase_number 1 (Style Negotiation)\n- phases[1] = phase_number 2 (Profile Building)\n- phases[2] = phase_number 3 (Skills Curation)\n- phases[3] = phase_number 4 (History Formatting)\n- phases[4] = phase_number 5 (Credentials Formatting)\n- phases[5] = phase_number 6 (Cover Letter Writing)\n- phases[6] = phase_number 7 (Style Review)\n- phases[7] = phase_number 8 (Integrity Check) — already has data\n- phases[8] = phase_number 9 (Document Formatting) — this is where we are now\n\nThe integrity check was already run and failed with 15 unsupported claims, but the user is triggering it again now. Even though the current phase is 9 and the previous integrity check completed, they're re-running the check fresh.\n\nNow I need to validate all the claims in the assembled CV against the raw source data and gap analysis. Looking at the raw CV, I can see Sarah Jenkins' education history from La Trobe University and her work experience at Bunnings Warehouse starting in March 2024.\n\nThe gap analysis reveals a comprehensive list of 19 identified gaps, ranging from quality control principles and MS Office proficiency to lab experience, LIMS systems, and NATA accreditation knowledge. Her First Aid Certificate has lapsed since 2022, and there are significant gaps around medical terminology and biochemistry panels. I need to check how the assembled CV addresses these gaps and whether the candidate evidence provided has been properly adjudicated.\n\nLooking at the profile section, I'm validating the claims made against the raw CV data. The opening statement about being a Biomedical Science graduate from La Trobe University in 2023 checks out against the documented degree completion dates of 2020-2023.\n\nHowever, the claim about laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety is problematic. While the CV confirms the degree itself, it doesn't explicitly state these specific lab skills anywhere. This appears to be an inference about what a typical Biomedical Science degree would include rather than something the candidate actually stated. Under the v3.1 rules, this doesn't qualify as a fair generalization of a stated fact—it's an unstated assumption about the degree's content.\n\nThe gap_analysis.json marks this requirement as \"Met\" based on the analyst's inference that the curriculum covers lab practices, but that assessment doesn't constitute accepted session evidence or direct CV content. The integrity check needs to validate against what's actually documented in cv_raw.txt, not what the analyst inferred about the degree program. The analyst inferred these details rather than drawing them from what the candidate actually stated, which violates the rule against pure inference as evidence. Moving through the remaining claims: biochemistry test panels, quality control principles, and medical terminology all get flagged as fabricated skills since their adjudications were skipped. The cash handling claim at Coles from January 2020 to February 2024 does pass verification with the cv showing over four years in that role, and the requirement for numerical accuracy is a reasonable generalization from that experience.\n\nFor the retail shift work claim, combining Bunnings (roughly two years from March 2024 onward) with the prior Coles tenure gives approximately six years total, which aligns with what's documented. However, the specific assertion about rotating rosters and weekend work isn't explicitly stated in the cv—it's an assumption about what retail work entails rather than something the candidate confirmed. But I'm noticing the gap analysis might have already addressed this requirement elsewhere, so I need to check what evidence was actually recorded there.\n\nMoving into the skills section, I'm finding that several technical competencies like laboratory pipetting, biosafety protocols, and specific biochemistry test panels aren't mentioned anywhere in the raw CV. These appear to be inferences drawn from the candidate's degree rather than demonstrated experience they've actually listed.\n\nThe same pattern continues with quality control principles, medical terminology, LIMS systems, and automated analyzer platforms—all marked as fabricated skills since they were skipped during the analysis. For data validation and numerical accuracy, I'm considering whether cash handling experience from their retail work could reasonably generalize to this skill, though the connection feels like a stretch. NATA accreditation standards is marked as fabricated since it was skipped in the gap assessment. Moving through the soft skills now—attention to detail and accuracy clearly connect to the cash handling work, teamwork is explicitly mentioned as \"team player,\" and verbal and written communication are fair generalizations from customer service roles. Roster flexibility isn't documented anywhere, so that's a gap. Reliability is directly stated in the CV, but adaptability to new systems appears to be cut off mid-evaluation. \"Adaptability to new systems and protocols\" is a soft skill that reasonably follows from working across multiple retail environments, so I'll pass that. But \"roster flexibility (rotating shifts, weekends)\" is a specific detail that doesn't appear anywhere in the CV—that's fabricated. Moving into the career history section, I'm verifying the Bunnings Warehouse entry: the employer and \"Team Member\" position both check out against the source document. The EFTPOS inference holds up as a reasonable generalization for modern retail. Moving on to verify the Bunnings stock management bullet — shelving work does support inventory accuracy, so that's fair. Now checking the Coles position details, which align with the dates and role provided. Now checking the education credentials — both the Bachelor of Biomedical Science from La Trobe in 2023 and VCE from Eltham High School in 2019 check out. Moving into the cover letter, I'm verifying the opening statements about Melbourne Pathology's daily specimen volume, which comes from research and is exempt from the CV, along with the degree reference which is already confirmed. I'm flagging that the practical training details about pipetting and biosafety protocols aren't in the original CV, so I need to investigate further.\n\nIn the connection paragraph, I'm seeing claims about QC frameworks and Westgard-based result review that reference gaps in the CV — specifically gap_1 for QC principles and gap_15 for Westgard methodology. These appear to be fabricated skills not supported by the actual CV content, which is a significant issue.\n\nMoving through the rest of the assessment, the cash handling experience at Coles gets a pass as a fair generalization of protocol-driven accuracy under pressure, and the framing about how laboratory accuracy parallels retail data handling is persuasive context rather than a factual claim, so that passes too. The research paragraph references are exempt since they're drawn directly from Melbourne Pathology's documented orientation program and career pathway details.\n\nNow checking what's left: the candidate profile shows no publications, awards, grants, or certifications to verify, so nothing additional to flag there. But I'm finding some real problems in the degree claims — the statements about laboratory training in pipetting and specimen handling aren't supported in the CV itself, they're inferences from the degree type. Same issue with the claim about biochemistry test panels being covered in coursework, and the quality control principles statement appears to be unsupported as well. Continuing through the remaining skills and cover letter claims — I'm finding more fabricated gaps around automated analyzer platforms and NATA standards, plus additional items marked as inferred from the degree rather than explicitly stated in the CV. I'm seeing the breakdown of individual skill gaps from the profile and skills sections — things like pipetting, specimen handling, biosafety protocols, biochemistry panels, quality control, medical terminology, LIMS fundamentals, and automated analyzer experience are all flagged as either missing or fabricated claims.\n\nThe integrity check confirms the same 15 violations across all sources, so the assessment is consistent. Now I need to update the assembly state to reflect this failed validation. Writing the integrity check output... Continuing through the skills section, marking additional claims as fabricated—quality control procedures, medical terminology, LIMS fundamentals, specific analyzer platforms like Roche and Abbott systems, and NATA accreditation standards all lack supporting evidence in the provided materials. I'm reconsidering whether some of those NOT_FOUND claims could actually qualify under the v3.1 rule about fair generalizations. A Bachelor of Biomedical Science degree would reasonably encompass laboratory training in pipetting, specimen handling, and biosafety protocols—that's a fair generalization of what the degree entails, not pure inference. The distinction matters: restating what the candidate actually stated versus inferring something they never mentioned. The Integrity Checker operates independently from the Analyst's assessment, so I'm holding firm on these being NOT_FOUND — the degree is listed but the candidate never explicitly stated those lab skills, and without direct evidence or an accepted session, I can't infer them. Now I'm moving through the career history to verify nothing's been fabricated. The Bunnings register bullet mentions EFTPOS payments, which is a reasonable inference from \"working on the register\" since it's standard in modern retail, though I'm questioning whether naming the specific technology crosses the line into assertion.\n\nI'm settling on FAILED with 15 violations, matching my previous assessment. Now I need to update the state file — I'll do a read-modify-write to update phases[7] with the fresh results and refresh the timestamps. I need to read the existing JSON file and modify it in place rather than reconstructing it from scratch. I'll update the phases[7].data with fresh integrity check results while preserving the current state—keeping the status as \"COMPLETE\", the completed_at timestamp, and the current_phase at 9. Since completed_phases is already 8 from the previous run, I won't increment it again. I just need to refresh the last_updated timestamp to reflect this new check.\n\nNow I'm writing out the updated JSON file and preparing the failure summary with the specific issues found, starting with the missing source reference. I'm seeing nine more fabricated skills spread across the profile, skills section, and cover letter—things like biochemistry test panels, quality control principles, LIMS fundamentals, and specific analyzer platforms that weren't mentioned in the original materials. I'm reviewing the soft skills to verify they're fair generalizations rather than fabrications. \"Data validation and numerical accuracy\" works as a reasonable extension of cash handling experience, and the other soft skills like adaptability, communication, teamwork, attention to detail, and reliability all map back to specific claims in the CV, so I'll pass those through.\n\nThat brings me to a final count of 15 violations total—6 skills marked as not found and 9 that appear to be fabricated gaps. The assessment has failed, so I need to update the state and prepare the output with the corrected data.","lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.pb_reasoning","order":15,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.reviewer_reasoning","order":18,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.sc_reasoning","order":19,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.sn_reasoning","order":20,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":6,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":null,"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.sr_reasoning","order":21,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":null,"lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":4466,"currentPath":["{#}XqEIXOU:3890","{#}kU0B1wk","{#}tOg74f9"]},"controlSettings":{"placeholder":"Enter text...","group":"agentReasoning"},"name":"Agent Reasoning.ta_reasoning","order":22,"group":"Agent Reasoning","lastValue":"The user wants me to run tone validation. I'll start by reading the necessary files - cv_raw.txt, candidate_profile.json, project_meta.json, and attempt to read cover_letter_sample.txt.All four files loaded successfully. Now I'll proceed through the phases silently.\n\n**Phase 2: Seniority Assessment**\n\nTarget: UX/UI Designer — Digital Banking, Sector: Banking / Financial Services\n\nWork history from `candidate_profile.json`:\n1. Bright Pixel Studio — UX Designer (Contract), 2025-03 → Present — **relevant: true, is_employment: true**\n2. Neighbourhood Digital — UX Design Intern (Part-Time), 2024-09 → 2025-02 — **relevant: true, is_employment: true**\n3. Career Break — Primary Carer — 2021-01 → 2024-08 — **relevant: false, is_employment: false**\n4. Ashurst — Senior Associate — Corporate & Commercial, 2015-02 → 2020-12 — **relevant: false, is_employment: true** (legal background, not UX)\n5. Ashurst — Graduate-at-Law / Seasonal Clerk, 2014-02 → 2015-01 — **relevant: false, is_employment: true** (legal background, not UX)\n\nSo the relevant UX experience is just the recent contract role at Bright Pixel Studio spanning a few months.\n\nThere's also an internship at Neighbourhood Digital that lasted around five months, plus some pro bono work during the career break. This is clearly a career-changer from law with only two short UX roles, so she's positioning as a junior UX designer. The bootcamp finished in June 2024, which is about two years out from now, and since she's already landed professional UX work beyond just the bootcamp, I'd mark `is_recent_graduate: false` — she has actual shipped experience even if it's limited.\n\nFor the classification: she's got roughly eight months of combined relevant UX work, so `level: Junior` is right. The current title is UX Designer, and the sector is banking/financial services, which isn't academic or research-focused. That puts her in the `\"direct-practical\"` registration bucket.\n\nLooking at the CV language, the work history uses past tense throughout — even the current role at Bright Pixel Studio is written in past tense with verbs like \"Redesigned,\" \"Led,\" \"Conducted,\" and \"Managed.\" The voice is consistently active across all the bullets. Now I'm examining the bullet format structure. designer on a healthcare patient portal redesign, created wireframes and prototypes in Figma for a nonprofit website migration, and participated in design sprints with client presentations. The bullet points average around 14 words each, with a range from 8 to 22 words depending on the scope and detail of the project.\n\nThe writing style uses action verbs with objects and subordinate clauses, maintaining a semi-formal professional tone without first-person pronouns. The bullets are telegraphic rather than full sentences, with strong quantification in the UX roles and ownership-framed language throughout. The legal experience follows the same crisp register, emphasizing deal values and team management, while the cover letter shifts to a more conversational, narrative-driven voice with self-aware confidence.\n\nI'm noting some weaknesses: the skills section has vague entries and hedging language like \"Comfortable\" and parenthetical qualifications that undercut the otherwise strong ownership framing. The CV demonstrates clear ownership through action verbs like \"Led end-to-end UX\" and \"Managed own client relationships,\" with concrete examples like the council community engagement portal project showing full lifecycle ownership from stakeholder workshops to developer handoff.\n\nThe content leans systems-centric—design artifacts, processes, tools—though it does incorporate people elements. Her signature phrases like \"end-to-end UX,\" the specific metric \"task completion rate from 34% to 71%,\" and \"plain-English\" recur throughout and should be preserved. The cover letter uses an alignment-led persuasion strategy, connecting her non-traditional background directly to role requirements, particularly in financial services where she demonstrates understanding of regulatory constraints and the compliance-usability tension. She employs confident, slightly self-aware statements that make the narrative distinctive, though the thought trails off mid-sentence. Now I'm scanning for weak phrasing and hedging language. \"Background in contract negotiation and client management (transferable from legal career)\" reads defensively with that parenthetical qualifier — it undermines rather than reinforces the point. Similarly, \"Comfortable presenting design rationale\" uses a weak hedge when it should be action-driven, and \"Strong understanding of WCAG 2.1 AA\" is filler that should be backed by concrete work instead of just stated as a skill.\n\nI'm also noticing \"Experienced in translating complex business requirements\" leads with a weak phrasing device; flipping it to lead with the verb would be stronger. The \"working knowledge of HTML/CSS sufficient to collaborate\" construction is similarly defensive with its qualifiers. For the current role at Bright Pixel Studio, the past tense throughout is actually a standard CV convention for completed tasks, so that's not necessarily a problem unless there's inconsistency elsewhere. The skills section leans on \"Comfortable\" as a register choice, which I should flag as part of the overall hedging pattern.\n\nLooking at the real priorities for a UX/UI Designer role at a bank, the CV is solid overall, but I'm spotting two high-priority issues: \"Comfortable presenting design rationale to senior stakeholders\" uses a qualifier that undermines confidence in a professional context, and the parenthetical \"(transferable from legal career)\" in the skills section feels defensive about the career pivot rather than owning those capabilities directly.\n\nBeyond that, there are several medium-priority phrasing issues where weak constructions like \"Experienced in\" and \"Working knowledge... sufficient to\" minimize what should be presented as strengths. The competitive analysis bullet also needs concrete outcomes—right now it just lists an activity without showing what was learned or changed as a result. And the healthcare portal bullet starts passively when it should lead with the designer's actual contribution.\n\nI'm also noticing the skills section uses inconsistent phrasing patterns compared to the experience bullets—noun and adjective leads like \"Comfortable\" and \"Background in\" clash with the verb-forward style elsewhere. Let me pull out the most concrete issues with exact quotes and specific rewording suggestions to make the feedback actionable. direct collaboration with front-end developers. The competitive analysis and heuristic evaluations for those three client projects need concrete outcomes — what insights emerged or what decisions did they inform? Same issue with the healthcare portal work: \"supported\" glosses over the actual contribution, so I should specify what was owned or led. The Education section formatting also needs a look.\n\nThe date formats are all over the place — \"Completed June 2024\" versus \"2011 – 2013\" and \"2007 – 2010\" — so I'll standardize those. I'm also noticing the Skills section header mentions \"Comfortable presenting design rationale,\" which feels tentative compared to the confident tone elsewhere on the CV, though that's already flagged. The cover letter itself reads well with a strong, distinctive voice, so no major issues there.\n\nBut I'm spotting another pattern: \"Familiar with Agile/Scrum workflows\" uses the same weak qualifier I've already called out in other places. This \"Familiar with\" phrasing keeps appearing in the skills section and undermines the overall confidence.\n\nLooking at the severity breakdown now, I have 8 issues total — 2 high severity, 5 medium, and 1 low. That's actually a reasonable distribution for what's otherwise a solid CV. The problems are real but targeted, not systemic like some weaker applications I've seen.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether \"Comfortable presenting\" should stay high severity. For a UX/UI Designer role at a bank, it does undermine credibility in the skills section since that's read first, so I'll keep it flagged as high. The legal career parenthetical is medium — it's an aside that softens the impact but isn't pervasive enough to be critical.\n\nNow I have my final eight issues across high, medium, and low severity, and I'm ready to structure the JSON output with all the findings.\n\nWriting style_findings.json... They completed UX retraining in 2024 and have already delivered contract work, so not a recent graduate, but definitely Junior-level given only about 8 months of relevant UX experience. I'm also noting their writing style tends toward active voice, past tense, verb-led bullets averaging around 14 words, with a semi-formal tone and no first-person pronouns—they favor confident action verbs extended with participial phrases like \"Redesigned X, improving Y.\"\n\nLooking at their profile more closely, the experience section shows strong metric-backed achievements with consistent verb-led structure and solid ownership framing, but the skills section undermines this with hedged language like \"Comfortable,\" \"Working knowledge,\" and \"Familiar with.\" Their cover letter strikes a more conversational, narrative tone that's self-assertively confident. I'm also spotting some unnecessary defensive moves—like the parenthetical about transferable skills from their legal career, and an internship bullet that uses \"Supported\" when they likely contributed more substantially than that verb suggests. weeks\", and I'm also noting that I led the full UX process for a community engagement portal from initial stakeholder input through to handing off to developers. I'm flagging some language issues in the resume — specifically that phrases like \"comfortable\" undermine the confident tone I've established, and I should reframe things like \"presents design rationale to senior stakeholders\" more assertively as someone who actively leads design critiques across teams.\n\nI'm also catching that the defensive parenthetical about the legal career background weakens the claim unnecessarily — the CV already shows that history, so I should just state that I've applied contract negotiation and client management directly in my current UX work. Similarly, \"experienced in\" is too passive compared to the verb-driven language I'm using elsewhere, so I'd strengthen it to \"translates complex business requirements into user flows and wireframes\" and emphasize how I bridge stakeholder needs with design output.\n\nThe hedging language keeps appearing — \"working knowledge\" and \"familiar with\" both minimize what I actually do. For HTML/CSS, I should reframe it as proficiency that enables direct collaboration on responsive implementation rather than just \"sufficient to collaborate.\" And with Agile, I'm not just familiar with it; I actively practice sprint planning and backlog grooming in Jira across my current engagements, so that's the language I need.\n\nI'm also noticing the competitive analysis bullet lacks impact — it doesn't say what I found or how those findings shaped decisions. Same issue with the healthcare portal work where \"supported\" buries my actual contribution. I need to specify what I owned or delivered, not just that I was there helping.\n\nThere's also an inconsistency in how I'm formatting dates across the education section — mixing month-year for the bootcamp with year ranges for degrees. I should standardize that.\n\nNow I'm double-checking these quotes against the source documents to make sure I'm capturing the exact wording. I'm verifying the remaining claims from the CV, checking items 5 through 8 against the source material, and now looking at how the ownership framing is demonstrated through the council community engagement portal project. I need to handle the duplicate Ashurst entries carefully — there are two different roles under the same employer, so I should structure the role classification to distinguish between them by position rather than just keying by employer name alone, otherwise the server matching could cause a mismatch. Now I'll generate the JSON file with both Ashurst entries included.The tone_validator timed out. According to my instructions, I need to handle this case. The validator timed out, which is a tool execution error rather than a REJECT verdict. Since the validator failed to respond, I should proceed to Phase 8 - the retry limit is one re-validation attempt, but since the tool timed out (not a REJECT), I'll note the timeout and proceed to the completion message. Actually, let me re-read the instructions.\n\nThe instructions say:\n- \"If `verdict === \"APPROVE\"`:\" proceed to Phase 8.\n- \"If `verdict === \"FLAG\"`:\" non-blocking, proceed to Phase 8.\n- \"If `verdict === \"REJECT\"`:\" fix and re-validate once.\n\nA timeout is none of these - it's a tool failure. I could attempt once more since this is effectively a failed first attempt, but there's a risk of an infinite loop. The instructions specify calling it once and retrying once on REJECT, but a timeout isn't a REJECT—it's a tool error. I think I should just move forward to Phase 8 rather than risk another timeout.\n\nThe validator timed out, so I can't get a verdict. The file was written and the validator was called, but since it failed to respond, I'll proceed to Phase 8 and note the timeout without adding confusion. The completion message should still be output.The tone_validator timed out twice. Per the protocol, I should proceed after the retry — the retry is the limit. I'll proceed to Phase 8 and output the completion message.\n\nThe instructions say \"Proceed to Phase 8 regardless of the second verdict — the retry is the limit.\"\n\nSo I'll output the completion message now.","lastEventId":null},{"lastDataType":1,"controlType":"multilineText","lastChangedByWidgetId":null,"lastExecutionContext":{"eventId":2004,"currentPath":["{#}zljd2bB","{#}Cd4zUaV","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}UtkbqJi","{#}g9WDwlc","{#}UxR0HIM","{#}lCODt3X","{#}kuoikr8"]},"controlSettings":{"value":"","placeholder":"Enter text..."},"name":"Agent Reasoning.validator_r